-- Q5885: Data Protection Technologies [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-188, differential privacy''s epsilon parameter controls the privacy-utility trade-off. A smaller epsilon (0.1) adds more mathematical noise to query results, providing stronger individual privacy guarantees but reducing the precision of statistical analyses.
Wrong (A): This reverses the relationship. Higher epsilon values mean less noise and weaker privacy protection, not stronger safeguards. An epsilon of 1.0 provides weaker privacy than 0.1.
Wrong (C): Differential privacy is not binary. The epsilon parameter creates a continuous spectrum of privacy-utility trade-offs, and different values produce measurably different outcomes.
Wrong (D): This also reverses the relationship. An epsilon of 0.1 maximizes privacy protection with more noise, reducing (not maximizing) statistical utility.$EXPL$
